{"name":"Sugarcane Dahlia","hex":"#f7c2bf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sugarcane","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of sugar cane."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sugarcane"},{"term":"dahlia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Dahlia, tuberous perennial flowering plants native to Mexico."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dahlia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sugarcane","blocks":[{"text":"Sugarcane or sugar cane is a species of tall, perennial grass that is used for sugar production. The plants are 2–7 m tall with stout, jointed, fibrous stalks that are rich in sucrose, which accumulates in the stalk internodes. Sugarcanes belong to the grass family, Poaceae, an economically important flowering plant family that includes maize, wheat, rice, and sorghum, and many forage crops. It is native to New Guinea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Saccharum_officinarum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-125.jpg/330px-Saccharum_officinarum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-125.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugarcane","wikidata":"Q36940"},{"title":"Dahlia","blocks":[{"text":"Dahlia is a genus of bushy, tuberous, herbaceous perennial plants native to Mexico and Central America. Dahlias are members of the Asteraceae family of dicotyledonous plants, its relatives include the sunflower, daisy, chrysanthemum, and zinnia. There are 49 species of dahlia, with flowers in almost every hue, with hybrids commonly grown as garden plants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Dahlia_x_hybrida.jpg/330px-Dahlia_x_hybrida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dahlia","wikidata":"Q130918"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedi